## Abstract
In July 2026, archaeologists announced the landmark decipherment of inscriptions at Xultun, Guatemala, identifying the earliest known named scientist in the Americas. Within a 1,200-year-old scribal workshop, researchers recovered over 50 mathematical and astronomical "microtexts" explicitly credited to an 8th-century scholar named Sak Tahn Waax (also translated as Zactan B'aan). His wall equations demonstrated a flawless synchronization of the 260-day ritual calendar, the 365-day solar year, and the orbital synodic periods of Mars and Venus. While epigraphers have successfully mapped these glyphs, ancient mathematics has historically remained isolated from modern computer-aided verification. This article presents the first known machine-checked formalization of ancient Maya calendar systems using the Lean 4 interactive theorem prover. By encoding the astronomical computations, the base-20 positional Long Count calendar, and the multi-planetary synchronization matrix including the 780-day Mars cycle into dependent type theory, we bridge a millennium-scale gap between the mathematical scribes of the Petén rainforest and modern formal methods.
