## 1. Introduction: The Mathematics of Sak Tahn Waax
Western historiography frequently isolates mathematical lineages to Babylonia, Greece, and Western Europe. However, in the classic Maya lowlands, mathematical sciences reached an extraordinary apex of computational precision.
An excavation project led by archaeologist Franco Rossi of the [Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT)](https://dmse.mit.edu/news/dmse-researcher-identifies-first-known-maya-scientist/) uncovered an ancient ta'aj (scribe workshop) at the Xultun ruins in Petén, Guatemala. Using multispectral imaging to recover heavily eroded plaster-wall calculations, the team decoded "Text 19"—a unique 9-glyph configuration concluding with the phrase "cheheen" ("so says") followed by a personal signature. The text identifies Sak Tahn Waax ("White-Chested Fox"), making this the first time in history that an ancient Maya scientist has been credited by personal name alongside an original mathematical contribution.
The Maya conceptualized time as a complex series of interlocking cogwheels. A primary duty of these ancient calendar specialists was solving least common multiple (LCM) problems to track when distinct cosmic and ritual cycles would reset simultaneously. To demonstrate that these indigenous computational structures hold absolute mathematical validity under modern computational standards, we subject them to the axiomatic rigor of the Lean 4 proof assistant.
