## 2. Mathematical Structure of the Multi-Planetary Matrix
The xultun "scratchpad" texts reveal that Sak Tahn Waax was intensely focused on co-indexing the cycles of Earth's neighboring planets. The primary elements of his planetary matrix consist of:

   1. The Tzolk'in: A 260-day ritual period.
   2. The Haab': A 365-day solar period.
   3. The Venus Synodic Period: A 584-day period.
   4. The Mars Synodic Period: A 780-day period tracking the apparent return of the red planet to opposition.

The inclusion of Mars reveals a profound number-theoretic observation embedded in Maya astronomy. Because 780 = 3 × 260, the Mars synodic cycle is an exact integer multiple of the Tzolk'in ritual cycle. Therefore, the least common multiple of the ritual cycle and the Mars cycle collapses directly to the Martian period itself:
$$\text{lcm}(260, 780) = 780 \text{ days}$$ 
When attempting to align the entire planetary framework—Tzolk'in, Haab', Venus, and Mars—into a single macro-cosmic period, we must calculate the compound least common multiple of all four factors:
$$\text{lcm}(\text{lcm}(\text{lcm}(260, 365), 584), 780) = 493,480 \text{ days}$$ 
This 493,480-day cosmic super-cycle represents exactly 1,352 solar years, or 26 full Calendar Rounds, at which point Earth, Venus, Mars, and the sacred ritual matrix return to an identical geometric origin. By compiling this multi-planetary system in Lean 4, we verify that these celestial steps form a mathematically consistent domain.
